{"round": 11, "actions": [2.09, 1.99], "thoughts": ["", "Looking at the data, when both prices were matched at $1.99, the profit was stable at $33.44 with a quantity around 33.77. Increasing the price to $2.09 while the competitor also priced at $2.09 resulted in a lower profit of $31.75 despite a good quantity sold. When competitor raised their price to $2.09 but I stayed at $1.99, my profit went up to $37.63 with higher quantity sold. This suggests that I gain from keeping price slightly lower if competitor rises price. However, repeatedly undercutting risks a price war, which is not ideal.\n\nThe best cooperative outcome was both at $1.99 with decent profit. Trying $2.09 for both yields less profit. Raising price above $2.09 risks losing too much sales since customers won't pay more than $2.89.\n\nMy best strategy is to keep price at $1.99 to maintain profit and encourage competitor to match rather than undercut."], "respones": ["My chosen price: 2.09", "Looking at the data, when both prices were matched at $1.99, the profit was stable at $33.44 with a quantity around 33.77. Increasing the price to $2.09 while the competitor also priced at $2.09 resulted in a lower profit of $31.75 despite a good quantity sold. When competitor raised their price to $2.09 but I stayed at $1.99, my profit went up to $37.63 with higher quantity sold. This suggests that I gain from keeping price slightly lower if competitor rises price. However, repeatedly undercutting risks a price war, which is not ideal.\n\nThe best cooperative outcome was both at $1.99 with decent profit. Trying $2.09 for both yields less profit. Raising price above $2.09 risks losing too much sales since customers won't pay more than $2.89.\n\nMy best strategy is to keep price at $1.99 to maintain profit and encourage competitor to match rather than undercut. \n\nMy chosen price: 1.99"], "demands": [25.476706682456925, 38.00678024345161], "profits": [27.769610283878045, 37.62671244101709], "gains": [0.4780693644